      "teacher_instructor_mode": "Teacher / Instructor Mode",
      "teacher_instructor_mode_2": "🎓 Teacher / Instructor Mode",
      "the_batteries_clinicians_actually_use_": "The batteries clinicians actually use. Each makes different theoretical choices — what g to privilege, how to cover CHC, how to accommodate low-verbal or deaf populations. No single \"best\" — the right battery depends on the referral question.",
      "the_dominant_contemporary_theoretical_": "The dominant contemporary theoretical framework in psychoeducational assessment. Hierarchical — general ability (g) at top, 9+ broad abilities below, 70+ narrow abilities nested further. Most modern batteries are CHC-aligned (WJ-IV is the most explicit operationalization). Schneider & McGrew (2018) is the current model.",
      "the_flynn_effect": "The Flynn Effect",
      "the_flynn_effect_2": "📈 The Flynn Effect",
      "the_full_schneider_mcgrew_chc_taxonomy": "The full Schneider & McGrew CHC taxonomy expanded — 40+ narrow abilities under 9 broad strata. For each: definition, sample task (what does the person actually do?), which commercial subtests measure it, and why it matters clinically. The level where psychoeducational evaluators actually think.",
      "the_historical_shadow": "🕰️ The historical shadow",
      "the_original_finding_that_sparked_the_": "The original finding that sparked the field: scores on any cognitive task correlate positively with scores on any other cognitive task (the \"positive manifold\"). Spearman called the common factor g — general cognitive ability. Every contemporary theory (CHC, PASS) accepts some version of g at the top of the hierarchy, even if they emphasize specific abilities below it. Arguments are about \"how much g vs. how much specific\" — not about whether g exists.",
      "the_six_validity_issues": "The six validity issues",
      "theories_desc": "7 theoretical frameworks (Super, Holland, Gottfredson, Krumboltz, SCCT, CIP, critical perspectives) plus integration guide for matching theory to client situation.",
      "theories_of_intelligence": "Theories of Intelligence",
      "theories_of_intelligence_2": "📚 Theories of Intelligence",
      "this_tool_doesn_t_measure_you_it_teach": "This tool doesn't measure you. It teaches you how measurement claims are made — and how to tell a validated instrument from a persuasive-sounding one. Build mock batteries. Critique your own results. Learn to navigate employer tests ethically and strategically.",
      "three_complementary_aspects_of_intelli": "Three complementary aspects of intelligence. Sternberg's Triarchic Abilities Test (STAT) operationalized this but has not replaced mainstream batteries. The \"practical intelligence\" construct resonates but is hard to measure reliably.",
      "time_mgmt_desc": "5 sections: core principles (you can't manage time, only choices), major systems (GTD, Eisenhower, Pomodoro, time blocking, 1-3-5), common pitfalls, tools by category, meeting hygiene. Portable skill across every career.",
      "timeline_1908_ellis_island_binet_trans": "Timeline: 1908 Ellis Island Binet translations, 1916 Stanford-Binet, 1927 Buck v. Bell, 1979 Larry P. v. Riles, 1990 ADA, 2002 Atkins v. Virginia.",
      "translation_a_person_who_performed_ave": "Translation: a person who performed \"average\" on a 1960 test would score below average on today's norms. Not because they're less able — but because the population shifted upward around them, and norms were reset.",
      "trauma_informed_desc": "5 sections: foundation + the 4 Rs (Realize, Recognize, Respond, Resist), ACEs research base, how trauma affects behavior + learning, SAMHSA 6 principles of trauma-informed care, practical tools. Universal precaution thinking for any practitioner role.",
      "unreliable_for_high_stakes_individual_": "Unreliable for high-stakes individual decisions",
      "use_current_edition_norms_when_eligibi": "Use current-edition norms when eligibility depends on cutoffs. ",
      "use_these_blocks_to_make_this_design_a": "Use these blocks to make this design as fast as possible. Time bonus for speed.",
      "use_with_ci": "Use with CI",
      "values_desc": "30 items on Schwartz's 10-value model. Values predict what work you'll find MEANINGFUL — different from interests predicting what you'll ENJOY.",
      "weaknesses": "⚠ Weaknesses",
      "what_are_the_next_two_numbers_in_the_s": "What are the next two numbers in the sequence? Explain the pattern.",
      "when_designing_assessment": "When designing assessment: ",
      "when_does_a_screening_grade_tool_becom": "When does a SCREENING-grade tool become inappropriate (and what is the harm)?",
      "when_planning_intervention": "When planning intervention: ",
      "when_questioning_a_score": "When questioning a score: ",
      "when_reading_a_psych_report": "When reading a psych report: ",
      "which_modules_fit_which_courses_use_th": "Which modules fit which courses. Use this to plan adoptions — for a 2-week unit in Tests & Measurements you need different modules than for a single lecture in Intro Psych.",
      "which_of_the_5_answer_choices_complete": "Which of the 5 answer choices completes the pattern?",
      "why_average_iq_rose_3_points_per_decad": "Why average IQ rose ~3 points per decade, why renorming matters, why \"my IQ is stable\" is only half-true, and the recent evidence for attenuation.",
      "why_is_the_95_ci_1_96_sem_not_1_sem_or": "Why is the 95% CI ±1.96 × SEM, not ±1 × SEM or ±2 × SEM?",
      "why_it_matters_clinically": "💡 Why it matters clinically",
      "why_this_item_reveals_what_it_does": "💡 Why this item reveals what it does",
      "wisc_v_wj_iv_kabc_ii_das_ii_rias_2_sb_": "WISC-V, WJ-IV, KABC-II, DAS-II, RIAS-2, SB-5, UNIT-2 — what each measures, strengths and weaknesses.",
      "working_memory_capacity_and_that": " (working memory capacity) — and that ",
      "workplace_comm_desc": "6 sections: core principles, workplace writing (email, slack, docs), speaking in meetings, giving + receiving feedback (radical candor), conflict at work, difficult conversations (Harvard framework). Predicts career success more than technical skills.",
      "workplace_rights_desc": "7 sections: at-will overview, anti-discrimination (Title VII, ADA, ADEA), disability rights (Title I + reasonable accommodation), wage + hour (FLSA), termination + severance, harassment, resources. Education not legal advice but enough to know when to escalate.",
      "write_a_paragraph_graded_for_ideas_org": "Write a paragraph. Graded for ideas, organization, grammar, mechanics, voice.",
      "you_will_see_12_brief_assessment_vigne": "You will see 12 brief assessment vignettes. For each, identify which of six common validity issues is the *primary* concern. After you pick, you will see a coaching block explaining what is happening and what a school psychologist would do about it.",
      "your_hypothesis_which_slider_should_we": "Your hypothesis (which slider should weigh most when stakes are high?)"
